<https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#resource/docs/codebase/normalization-walkthrough.md> a owl:NamedIndividual,
        kw:ResourceSpecification ;
    rdfs:label "Build the canonical corpus vocabulary worked example" ;
    skos:definition "Source-reviewed task instructions for discovering, binding, checking and reviewing normalize without fabricating execution or provenance."@en ;
    kw:resourceKind "worked example" ;
    kw:resourceText """# An agent task: normalize a saved paper corpus

Author: Brent Thompson <brent.thompson@ucf.edu>

This is the detailed worked example for the operational ontology. It is a source-reviewed plan with
read-only fixture checks, not a claim that a new model run has been executed. The
[other stage worked tasks](stages/README.md) now extend this structure across the pipeline; many
internal helpers still have explicitly marked structural-only descriptions.

## Discover and bind

Start at `kw:CodebaseDocumentation`, follow `documentsWorkflow`, then `hasStep` to `kw:step/normalize`.
Read its English `skos:definition`, `contractJson`, `hasRecovery`, `hasCheck` and `hasDataBinding` links.
Follow the papers binding through `implementedBy` to `NormalizationAgent.run`, `hasArgument` to the
`papers: list[dict]` argument, and `bindsArtifact` to `papers/*.json`. This expresses the value binding;
it does not say the bound method opens the files itself.

`Run.papers()` loads sorted saved paper records; `src.run.normalize()` constructs
`NormalizationAgent(run.ledger)` and calls its `run(papers)` method. Use the CLI or wrapper invocation
for orchestration so credentials, settings, ledger and completion recording remain with the existing
runtime. Direct calls require reproducing that context and are not the default execution path.

Saved records are a different boundary from raw model-response JSON. A paper must identify its `key`
and contain concept/relation/causal/measurement lists. Concept IDs and labels must be usable strings.
Saved measurements reference the property concept through `concept`; the raw extraction schema names
that field `property`. The documented check validates this saved boundary, not an assumed raw schema.

## Assess before work

Run in the isolated documentation environment, from the repository root:

```powershell
outputs/development/codebase-docs/.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/ontology_agent.py guide --step normalize
outputs/development/codebase-docs/.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/ontology_agent.py assess --run si-topcon-n15-20261015-150628 --step normalize --phase before
```

The recipe comes from RDF. The second command reads an existing run without writing it. It checks
recorded extraction/readable products and the paper contract. Missing fields, duplicate paper/concept
IDs, malformed labels and unresolved within-paper endpoints block the proposed action. Partial
extraction is not accepted merely because the CLI saved a completion key.

`ready-for-runtime-conditions` means the saved data passed these bounded checks. It does not establish
live provider readiness, safe ownership, authorized write scope or permission to rebuild a shared store.
Resolve mode-specific prerequisites/conditions before using the existing trusted invocation template.

## Follow the implementation

`hasOperation` and `nextOperation` describe the reviewed source order: clean fields → lexical groups →
semantic merge review → canonical identities → type votes → claim aggregation → contradictions/scoring →
causal ordering → summary. Each operation links through `operationImplementation` to its source function.
The method mutates the supplied paper lists in memory; this stage's orchestrator does not rewrite the
saved `papers/*.json` inputs.

The returned dictionary has concepts, relations, causal, measurements, contradictions, causal_order,
summary, concept_lineage and internal stats. The orchestrator saves eight returned fields. `reports.corpus_report()`
writes Markdown and returns separate report-derived statistics for `normalized/corpus_stats.json`.
Do not serialize internal `norm.stats` as a substitute for the report-derived artifact.

## Assess after work and choose the next action

```powershell
outputs/development/codebase-docs/.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/ontology_agent.py assess --run si-topcon-n15-20261015-150628 --step normalize --phase after
```

The documentation checks require recorded completion, readable required outputs, documented field
types, unique canonical IDs, canonical endpoints and supporting-paper coverage. Artifact digests are
rechecked before returning. The evaluator is linked from each check through `evaluatedBy`; its bounded
predicate description is in `checkRule`, with CLI argument templates in `evaluationArguments`.

Even when structural checks pass, aliases, contradictory claims, type decisions and evidence need
review. The helper returns `review-required`, not scientific acceptance. These stricter agent boundary
checks do not change the pipeline's own runtime acceptance rules or repair source results.

`canAdvanceTo` identifies placement as the next conceptual stage; its own runtime/store conditions
still apply. If normalization is rerun, `invalidatesStep` identifies placement, enrichment, interop,
integration and reports for freshness review. It never deletes or reruns them automatically.

New normalizations persist the exact `(paper key, original concept ID) → canonical ID` mapping in
`normalized/concept_lineage.json`. Canonical `label_sources` preserve original spellings and paper
identities through enrichment and OWL label export. Older saved runs, including the n15 worked corpus,
lack this sidecar; aliases and supporting papers cannot reconstruct every reviewed merge decision.

## Reviewer navigation

Open the local WebVOWL preview at http://127.0.0.1:8058/. Normalization opens with a bounded data-flow
graph and an inspector containing its `skos:definition`, bindings, checks and recovery. Switch to
implementation for functions/substeps; use code lookup for bounded neighborhoods instead of rendering
all records at once. Artifact connections expose producers, consumers and bindings in both directions.
Function records expose source lines, signatures, formal arguments/defaults and return annotations.
Unannotated types and unreviewed behavior remain visible gaps.

On smaller windows the inspector stacks beneath the graph; **Selected record** and **Back to graph**
provide direct navigation. Source links open generated, fingerprinted code snapshots rather than
unregistered public ontology IRIs. Full graph JSON remains downloadable, with projection semantics
explicitly separated from the original formal RDF types.
""" ;
    kw:sourceDigest "811612fa8344feda3830818630e2bb8ca1ac46c1ddf1a534e06558f33d6d3801" ;
    kw:sourcePath "docs/codebase/normalization-walkthrough.md" .

<https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#resource/docs/codebase/stages/enrich.md> a owl:NamedIndividual,
        kw:ResourceSpecification ;
    rdfs:label "Add definitions, restrictions and candidates worked example" ;
    skos:definition "Source-reviewed task instructions for discovering, binding, checking and reviewing enrich without fabricating execution or provenance."@en ;
    kw:resourceKind "worked example" ;
    kw:resourceText """# Worked task: Add definitions, restrictions and candidates

Author: Brent Thompson <brent.thompson@ucf.edu>

This is a source-reviewed operational recipe, not a claim that a new model or literature run was executed. The ontology embeds this text and fingerprints the source. Scientific acceptance and live readiness remain separate checks.

## Research task and discovery

Placed TOPCon classes have stable IDs and parents. Read them with normalized scientific and causal relations, then discover EnrichmentAgent.run(classes,relations,causal) and its two-element return tuple.

Start at `kw:CodebaseDocumentation` → `documentsWorkflow` → `hasStep` → `kw:step/enrich`. Read its `skos:definition`, `contractJson`, `hasDataBinding`, `hasOperation`, `hasCheck` and `hasRecovery` links. Follow `implementedBy`/`operationImplementation` to source callables, `hasArgument` to formal names/types/defaults and `bindsArtifact` to source/output artifact specifications. Artifact incoming links identify producers and consumers.

## Bind and initialize

Placed class records and normalized relation/causal lists are independent arguments. Only non-excluded classes are enriched, but all class identities are returned.

| Input | Required shape or origin |
|---|---|
| `classes[]` | placed canonical fields including id,label,parent,excluded,alt_labels,definitions,iri |
| `relations[]` | canonical s,p,o and evidence/support |
| `causal[]` | canonical cause,effect,polarity and evidence/support |

Require the shared ontology store, construct EnrichmentAgent(run.ledger), then call run(classes,relations,causal) via the existing stage.

Side effects: Mutates live class dictionaries in memory: initializes definitions/restrictions/disjointness, adds candidates and annotations. The orchestrator saves enriched classes separately from placement classes.

### Trusted execution entry points

The following are **unbound argument-vector templates** from workflow.json. Resolve every uppercase placeholder using actual authorized selections; do not execute these literal placeholders. Prefer the existing controller to direct callable invocation. `ontology_agent.py plan` can bind/check templates without executing them. Credentials remain in the runtime.

CLI template:

```json
[
  "uv",
  "run",
  "--with-requirements",
  "requirements.txt",
  "python",
  "-m",
  "src.run",
  "enrich",
  "--run-id",
  "RUN_ID",
  "--no-figures"
]
```

WRAPPER template:

```json
[
  "outputs/dashboard/.venv/Scripts/python.exe",
  "-m",
  "dpv_dashboard.worker",
  "PROJECT_ID",
  "stage",
  "--domain",
  "DOMAIN_ID",
  "--stage",
  "enrich"
]
```

### Data bindings

| Direction | Saved or explicit input | Selector → callable value | Adaptation |
|---|---|---|---|
| input | `ontology/classes.json` | `$` → `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent.run` / `classes` | Read the corresponding saved JSON value; pass classes and normalized claims as separate arguments, not a combined ontology graph. |
| input | `normalized/relations.json` | `$` → `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent.run` / `relations` | Read the corresponding saved JSON value; pass classes and normalized claims as separate arguments, not a combined ontology graph. |
| input | `normalized/causal.json` | `$` → `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent.run` / `causal` | Read the corresponding saved JSON value; pass classes and normalized claims as separate arguments, not a combined ontology graph. |
| output | `ontology/enriched.json` | `$` → `src/run.py:enrich` / `classes` | Unpack the agent tuple (classes, properties), then serialize each component. |
| output | `ontology/properties.json` | `$` → `src/run.py:enrich` / `properties` | Unpack the agent tuple (classes, properties), then serialize each component. |
| output | `ontology/materials_project.json` | `$` → `src/run.py:enrich` / `agent.materials_log` | Persist optional Materials Project lookup/annotation results and review provenance. |
| output | `ontology/review_issues.json` | `$` → `src/run.py:enrich` / `ontology_review.issues(classes)` | Refresh the shared class review findings from enriched classes. |
| output | `ontology/cross_domain_candidates.json` | `$` → `src/run.py:enrich` / `ontology_review.correspondences(...)` | Advisory historical correspondences; these do not replace explicit source-run selection for integration. |

## Follow the source operations

The sequence describes the implemented plan. Profile-dependent branches and guarded optional operations are stated explicitly; it does not assert that every branch runs in every attempt.

1. **Initialize live class enrichment** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent.run`. Retain excluded records, initialize live definitions/restrictions/disjointness and determine whether the configured profile permits model-knowledge definitions.

2. **Gather mapping candidates** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent._candidates`. Combine store candidates, bounded portal searches and propagated mappings, preserving source, score, path and label-match distinctions.

3. **Add optional material annotations** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent._materials`. Annotate applicable material classes using configured Materials Project support, retaining lookup evidence and review results.

4. **Translate evidenced causal claims** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent._causal`. Create local and appropriate RO/CCO causal restrictions for eligible evidence-backed canonical claims.

5. **Resolve scientific property candidates** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent._property_candidates`. Retrieve object properties for evidenced non-is_a relations and retain only candidates fitting domain and range.

6. **Commit deterministic predicate matches** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent._predicate_restrictions`. Use exact or configured tie-broken property matches directly, leaving other eligible relations for model review.

7. **Apply profile-specific enrichment** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent._apply`. Apply local split-pass or frontier combined answers; retain definition basis/status, validate restrictions against source relations and flag unsupported results.

8. **Check disjointness and persist tuple** — `src/agents/enrichment.py:EnrichmentAgent._local_properties`. After dropping disjointness conflicting with paper is_a, build local property descriptions. The orchestrator unpacks classes/properties and saves sidecars.

## Handle the return and persistence

- tuple[classes, properties dictionary]
- agent.materials_log and agent.doubts are saved separately

Unpack classes,properties. Preserve all class identities, but require definition/restriction fields only for live classes. Generated, evidence-draft and supported definitions have different authority; external candidates are not committed mappings.

## Assess and choose the next action

Run the read-only helper from the repository root in the documentation environment. `RUN_FOLDER` means an existing run-relative directory under the selected outputs root, not a guessed future run. Guide retrieval requires no run folder. Use the integration folder for integration, the report folder or domain root for reports, and the domain attempt for other stages.

```powershell
outputs/development/codebase-docs/.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/ontology_agent.py guide --step enrich
outputs/development/codebase-docs/.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/ontology_agent.py assess --run RUN_FOLDER --step enrich --phase before
outputs/development/codebase-docs/.venv/Scripts/python.exe scripts/ontology_agent.py assess --run RUN_FOLDER --step enrich --phase after
```

The helper never runs pipeline commands or repairs data. `blocked` stops the proposed advance; `runtime-check` requires real environment/scope checks; `review` requires evidence or domain review. A successful bounded predicate does not certify all schema fields or scientific meaning. File hashes are rechecked to detect changes during observation.

| Check | Phase | Scope |
|---|---|---|
| `shape-ontology-enriched` (shape) | after | ontology/enriched.json |
| `review-findings` (scientific_review) | after | Generated definitions, imported definitions and evidence-supported definitions have different authority. |
| `placed-input` (shape) | before | ontology/classes.json |
| `properties-object` (shape) | after | ontology/properties.json |
| `placed-enriched-coverage` (identity_coverage) | after | ontology/classes.json |
| `restriction-targets` (references) | after | ontology/enriched.json |
| `disjoint-targets` (references) | after | ontology/enriched.json |
| `canonical-input-endpoints` (canonical_references) | before | Saved joins |
| `placed-input-parents` (references) | before | ontology/classes.json |

Completion rule: Confirm expected artifacts are parseable and inspect the saved stage/error records. Review content; file presence alone is not completion.

Recovery: Inspect unsupported definitions, failed rows and restrictions dropped; reattempt enrichment and regenerate interop/integration/reports from the new results.

Limit: Generated definitions, imported definitions and evidence-supported definitions have different authority.

Follow `canAdvanceTo` to the next conceptual stage and inspect its mode-specific prerequisites. A rerun requires freshness review of stages linked by `invalidatesStep`; the documentation helper never deletes or reruns them. For reports, completion ends this task; integration is optional when only one domain exists.

## WebVOWL review

At http://127.0.0.1:8058/, select **Add definitions and formal relationships** (its shortened tab label may differ). Start with Data flow to trace saved values, switch to Implementation for source operations, and select a function for its definition, formal arguments and fingerprinted source lines. The stage inspector links this worked example. Checks show before/after scope; runtime-only conditions remain visible. Use bounded Code lookup for connected records instead of loading all code nodes at once.
""" ;
    kw:sourceDigest "b126a203a0411c3aa1955253c90e40f3bcc59745ad80e19292b1dcbaa9bb8956" ;
    kw:sourcePath "docs/codebase/stages/enrich.md" .

<https://w3id.org/ucf-dpv/knowledge-workflow/codebase#resource/docs/codebase/stages/extract.md> a owl:NamedIndividual,
        kw:ResourceSpecification ;
    rdfs:label "Create evidence-backed per-paper records worked example" ;
    skos:definition "Source-reviewed task instructions for discovering, binding, checking and reviewing extract without fabricating execution or provenance."@en ;
    kw:resourceKind "worked example" ;
    kw:resourceText """# Worked task: Create evidence-backed per-paper records

Author: Brent Thompson <brent.thompson@ucf.edu>

This is a source-reviewed operational recipe, not a claim that a new model or literature run was executed. The ontology embeds this text and fingerprints the source. Scientific acceptance and live readiness remain separate checks.

## Research task and discovery

A selected paper contains TOPCon device concepts, reported efficiency measurements and a captioned figure. The orchestrator combines source metadata with parser output before supplying one paper dictionary to ExtractionAgent.run.

Start at `kw:CodebaseDocumentation` → `documentsWorkflow` → `hasStep` → `kw:step/extract`. Read its `skos:definition`, `contractJson`, `hasDataBinding`, `hasOperation`, `hasCheck` and `hasRecovery` links. Follow `implementedBy`/`operationImplementation` to source callables, `hasArgument` to formal names/types/defaults and `bindsArtifact` to source/output artifact specifications. Artifact incoming links identify producers and consumers.

## Bind and initialize

One parsed paper dictionary, including source metadata, sections and figures, is passed to the agent. The saved paper record is composed by the orchestrator, not returned wholesale by the agent.

| Input | Required shape or origin |
|---|---|
| `paper` | {key,title,sections:[{heading,text}],figures:[{id,caption}], source metadata} |
| `saved measurements` | concept stores the property concept ID; entity is optional |

Instantiate ExtractionAgent(run.ledger) under the configured profile. Use CLI extract for combined selection/parsing/extraction or wrapper extract after separately completed ingestion/parsing.

Side effects: The agent writes extraction cache and ledger records. src.run.extract writes finalized papers and updates corpus selection/failures; wrapper raises after partial extraction.

### Trusted execution entry points

The following are **unbound argument-vector templates** from workflow.json. Resolve every uppercase placeholder using actual authorized selections; do not execute these literal placeholders. Prefer the existing controller to direct callable invocation. `ontology_agent.py plan` can bind/check templates without executing them. Credentials remain in the runtime.

CLI template:

```json
[
